The concept of inherent worth plays a crucial role in shaping the interpretation and application of rights and freedoms within the framework of European human rights law. Inherent worth refers to the intrinsic value of every individual, which underpins the belief that all people are entitled to certain fundamental rights simply by virtue of being human. This principle is foundational to the European Convention on Human Rights (ECHR) and informs various aspects of human rights jurisprudence.

